From f80a6ae41bcc68d7608c28247992eeb4014ef463 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 21:20:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Let arrays track their named properties by shape

Shape eligibility was an exact GetType() == typeof(JSObject) test in six
places, so a JSArray, a JSFunction and every built-in exotic had no shape and
therefore no inline-cache entry for any named property. The exclusion is total:
measured with --cache-metrics, 0 hits out of 200 000 for a named read on an
array, the same for the write side, the same for a named read on a function
whether sloppy, strict or a class static, and the same for a typed array.

Make the gate a virtual SupportsShapeTracking - the pattern the class already
uses for SupportsOrdinaryIndexedWrite - and opt JSArray in. A named read on an
array goes 0 -> 199 999 hits and the write side 0 -> 199 999. Wall clock on a
10 M-iteration loop reading and writing one named property, same build with
only the override differing, eight interleaved pairs: median of paired ratios
0.93, seven of eight the same direction and one pair 11% against, so
directional rather than a figure. The hit-rate numbers are the exact ones.

It is an opt-IN, and the remarks say what a subclass has to earn: while an
object is in shape mode, the shape's tracked keys must be its complete set of
own named properties. GetPrototypeLookupShapeId reads "key absent from the
shape" as "no own property shadows the prototype's", and the transition entry
reads it as "creating this key is safe", so a subclass that breaks it does not
merely fail to help - it breaks both. An array earns it by having no named
property of its own written with a bare ownProperties.Put: its only exotic
named property is `length`, which is served from _length rather than stored.

Which is also why this does nothing for a.length, and cannot: a property
computed from the element store has no slot to live in, and should not, since
its value moves whenever the array does. It stays at 0 / 200 000 and is now
pinned by a test so the absence reads as designed. Elements are likewise
untouched and remain outside the shape.

JSFunction is deliberately NOT opted in, and would be a correctness bug rather
than a no-op: it installs length, name and prototype with bare
ownProperties.Put calls that never reach TrackShapeDataProperty, so its shape
would claim a key set missing three keys every function has. A scratch build
confirmed the hazard is currently masked only by accident - every function row
reported one dictionary fallback, so the shape was abandoned before anything
could trust it. That matters because the idiom that pays is statics on a
constructor function, which is what DeltaBlue reads in its hot path at 601x
off; the roadmap carries it as item 2-8 with its prerequisites in order.

Twelve tests in PropertyShapeCacheTests, including the bound on what this
buys: growing an array through a built-in reaches GetOwnProperties(create:
true), which abandons the shape by design because a mutable ref handed to
another assembly could add a named property without telling the tracker. One
fallback for five pushes, correctness unaffected, hits gone - measured and
pinned rather than found later.
